{"name":"Golden Coin","hex":"#fcd975","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"coin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of currency, usually metallic and in the shape of a disc, but sometimes polygonal, or with a hole in the middle.","A token used in a special establishment like a casino."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make of a definite fineness, and convert into coins, as a mass of metal.","To make or fabricate (especially a word or phrase)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold coin","blocks":[{"text":"A gold coin is a coin that is made mostly or entirely of gold. Most gold coins minted since 1800 are 90.0–92.0 % gold (22‑karat), while most of today's gold bullion coins are pure gold, such as the Britannia, Canadian Maple Leaf, Vienna Philharmonic, and American Buffalo. Alloyed gold coins, like the American Gold Eagle and South African Krugerrand, are typically 91.7 ‰ gold by weight, with the remainder being silver and copper."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Dubai_gold_coins.jpg/330px-Dubai_gold_coins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_coin","wikidata":"Q860641"},{"title":"Coin","blocks":[{"text":"A coin is a small object, usually round and flat, used primarily as a medium of exchange or legal tender. They are standardized in weight, and produced in large quantities at a mint in order to facilitate trade. They are most often issued by a government. Coins often have images, numerals, or text on them. The faces of coins or medals are sometimes called the obverse and the reverse, referring to the front and back sides, respectively. The obverse of a coin is commonly called heads, because it often depicts the head of a prominent person, and the reverse is known as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Kiloware.JPG/330px-Kiloware.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coin","wikidata":"Q41207"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Achaemenid_coin_daric_420BC_front.jpg/500px-Achaemenid_coin_daric_420BC_front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Achaemenid_coin_daric_420BC_front.jpg/500px-Achaemenid_coin_daric_420BC_front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Persian Achaemenid Daric, c. 490 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAchaemenid_coin_daric_420BC_front.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Gold_Solidus_of_Valentinian_II_-_obverse_YORYM_1998_853.jpg/500px-Gold_Solidus_of_Valentinian_II_-_obverse_YORYM_1998_853.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Gold_Solidus_of_Valentinian_II_-_obverse_YORYM_1998_853.jpg/1280px-Gold_Solidus_of_Valentinian_II_-_obverse_YORYM_1998_853.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c